insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('discounting-exercise-1', 'video', 'Discounting Exercise 1', 'Walks through a present value exercise: setting up a flexible model with
dynamic cell references, computing a discount factor manually as 1/(1+r)^t,
and linking the result back to the answer cell.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297064?dnt=1', 347, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('discounting-exercise-1--read', 'read', 'Discounting Exercise 1: reading', 'Walks through a present value exercise: setting up a flexible model with
dynamic cell references, computing a discount factor manually as 1/(1+r)^t,
and linking the result back to the answer cell.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## Setting up the model

Start by identifying your dynamic inputs — the cash flow and discount
rate — and make sure every formula references those cells directly.
Never hard-code the numbers. Using cell references means you can reset
the inputs and the entire model updates automatically.

Reserve a separate work area below the inputs. Build your calculations
there, then link the final result back to the answer cell with a simple
reference. This keeps your model readable and easy to audit.

## The discount factor approach

Rather than writing present value as a single formula, break it into
two columns: the present value of one dollar, and the present value of
the payment.

The present value of one dollar arriving at time t is the discount
factor: 1 divided by (1 + r) raised to the power t. This number is
always less than one — it tells you what a future dollar is worth
today. Multiply it by the cash flow to get the present value of the
payment.

## Anchoring references with F4

When you reference the discount rate and cash flow cells, press F4
immediately after clicking the cell. This locks the reference with
dollar signs ($B$2 style) so it stays fixed if you copy the formula
across periods. Leave the period (year) reference unanchored so it
increments correctly as you extend the model to additional cash flows.

## Checking your answer

Once your model is complete, link the answer cell to the present value
of the payment in your work area. A correct answer highlights green;
an incorrect one highlights red, signalling a formula or reference
error to investigate.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'discounting-exercise-1'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
