-- discounting-exercise-2
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('discounting-exercise-2', 'video', 'Discounting Exercise 2', 'Walks through finding the present value of a series of cash flows by using
SEQUENCE to generate period numbers, building a discount factor column, and
summing the individual PVs with SUM and Ctrl+D to fill down.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297093?dnt=1', 198, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('discounting-exercise-2--read', 'read', 'Discounting Exercise 2: reading', 'Walks through finding the present value of a series of cash flows by using
SEQUENCE to generate period numbers, building a discount factor column, and
summing the individual PVs with SUM and Ctrl+D to fill down.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## Present value of a series of cash flows

The present value of a series of cash flows is the sum of the individual
present values of each payment. To calculate this in Excel, set up three
columns: the period number, the discount factor, and the present value of
each payment. Then sum the present value column for your final answer.

## Using SEQUENCE to generate period numbers

Rather than typing 1 through 7 by hand, use SEQUENCE to generate the period
numbers automatically. SEQUENCE takes four arguments: rows, columns, start,
and step. For seven annual periods starting at year one, enter
=SEQUENCE(7,1,1,1). The columns, start, and step arguments all default to 1,
so they could be omitted, but spelling them out makes the logic explicit.

## Building the discount factor

The discount factor for period n is 1 divided by (1 + rate) raised to the
power n. Lock the discount rate cell with F4 so it stays fixed when you copy
the formula down, but leave the period cell reference relative so it
increments with each row. Multiply the discount factor by the cash flow
(also locked with F4) to get the present value of each payment.

## Filling down and summing

Select the three formula cells in the first row, then extend the selection
to the bottom of your range using Ctrl+Shift+Down, and press Ctrl+D to fill
down. Finally, use SUM over the present value column to add up all individual
present values and arrive at the total present value of the series.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'discounting-exercise-2'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
