-- discounting-exercise-4
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('discounting-exercise-4', 'video', 'Discounting Exercise 4', 'Walks through Exercise 4: building a model to calculate the future value of a
series of cash flows by compounding each cash flow individually using the
FV formula, SEQUENCE, and SUM, then adding the results together.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297243?dnt=1', 275, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('discounting-exercise-4--read', 'read', 'Discounting Exercise 4: reading', 'Walks through Exercise 4: building a model to calculate the future value of a
series of cash flows by compounding each cash flow individually using the
FV formula, SEQUENCE, and SUM, then adding the results together.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## Future value of a series of cash flows

When you have multiple cash flows at different points in time, the total
future value is simply the sum of each individual cash flow compounded
forward to the same end date. The key insight is that each cash flow
compounds forward a different number of periods depending on when it occurs.

## The T minus t rule

If your end date is period T (say, year 10) and a cash flow occurs in
period t (say, year 3), you compound it forward T minus t periods (7 years,
not 10). The future value of that cash flow is: payment × (1 + r)^(T − t).
Cash flows early in the timeline compound the most; cash flows near the end
compound very little.

## Building the model

Use SEQUENCE to generate period labels starting at zero. Anchor your
payment cell with dollar signs ($B$2) so it stays fixed when you copy
the formula down, but leave the period cell relative so the exponent
updates row by row.

Once the future value of a single payment is set up correctly, select
the block with Ctrl+Shift+Up, then press Ctrl+D to copy the formula
down the entire column in one step.

## Checking your work

The compounding factor column should decrease as you move down — earlier
cash flows have more periods to compound, later ones have fewer. If the
column is flat or increasing, check your exponent formula. The final
answer is a SUM of the future value column.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'discounting-exercise-4'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
