-- pv-and-cell-references
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('pv-and-cell-references', 'video', 'PV And Cell References', 'Introduces the PV function for valuing an annuity and explains why
cell references — including mixed absolute/relative references using F4 —
are essential for building flexible, dynamic financial models.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297298?dnt=1', 507, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('pv-and-cell-references--read', 'read', 'PV And Cell References: reading', 'Introduces the PV function for valuing an annuity and explains why
cell references — including mixed absolute/relative references using F4 —
are essential for building flexible, dynamic financial models.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## The PV function

The PV function calculates the present value of a stream of equal cash
flows — an annuity. It takes three required arguments: rate (the
discount rate per period), nper (the number of periods), and pmt (the
payment each period). For a $100 annuity over 10 years at 5%, the
formula is =PV(rate, nper, pmt).

One common surprise: Excel returns a negative number when you enter a
positive payment. This reflects Excel''s sign convention — a positive
cash inflow today corresponds to a negative outflow in the future. To
get a positive result, place a minus sign in front of the pmt argument.

## Use cell references, not hard-coded numbers

Never type numbers directly into your formulas. Instead, put your
inputs — rate, periods, cash flow — in dedicated cells and reference
those cells in every formula. This keeps your model flexible: change
one input cell and every formula updates automatically.

## Absolute and mixed cell references

When you copy a formula across rows and columns, relative references
shift with the formula, which breaks it. Fix this with dollar signs.
A fully locked reference like $D$3 never moves. A mixed reference
like D$8 locks the row but lets the column shift — useful when your
rates run across columns. A reference like $C9 locks the column but
lets the row shift — useful when your periods run down rows.

## The F4 shortcut

Rather than typing dollar signs manually, press F4 after clicking a
cell reference in the formula bar. Each press cycles through the four
locking states: fully locked ($D$3), row-only (D$3), column-only
($D3), and fully unlocked (D3). Using F4 is faster and avoids typos.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'pv-and-cell-references'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
