-- flags
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('flags', 'video', 'Flags', 'Introduces flag logic as an alternative to nested IF/AND formulas,
using binary 1/0 multipliers to apply conditional cash flows across
annuity periods.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297468?dnt=1', 387, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('flags--read', 'read', 'Flags: reading', 'Introduces flag logic as an alternative to nested IF/AND formulas,
using binary 1/0 multipliers to apply conditional cash flows across
annuity periods.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## What flag logic is

A flag is a helper value that equals 1 when a condition is true and
0 when it is false. Instead of wrapping your cash flow formula in a
nested IF/AND statement, you build a dedicated flag row, then
multiply your cash flow amount by that flag. When the flag is 1 the
cash flow passes through unchanged; when it is 0 the result is zero.
This keeps formulas short and makes your logic easy to audit.

## Converting TRUE and FALSE to 1 and 0

Excel treats TRUE and FALSE as text-like values in some contexts,
not as numbers. To force them into 1 and 0, multiply the logical
expression by 1. For example: =(B5>=$B$2)*1 returns 1 when the year
in B5 is at or after the start year in B2, and 0 otherwise. Anchoring
the start year cell with dollar signs lets you copy the formula down
the column without the reference drifting.

## Combining two flags

When you need a between condition — year is on or after the start AND
on or before the end — build two separate flag rows first. A start
flag fires once the year reaches the start year; an end flag fires
while the year is still within the end year. Multiply the two flags
together to get a combined flag. Because 1×1=1 and anything times
0 equals 0, the combined flag is only 1 when both conditions are
satisfied simultaneously. Your final cash flow row is simply the
combined flag multiplied by the cash flow amount.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'flags'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
