-- annuities-exercise-1
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('annuities-exercise-1', 'video', 'Annuities Exercise 1', 'Walks through solving an ordinary annuity exercise three ways: using
Excel''s PV function, a manual mathematical formula, and a worksheet
model with flags and conditional logic to handle variable cash flow periods.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297508?dnt=1', 518, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('annuities-exercise-1--read', 'read', 'Annuities Exercise 1: reading', 'Walks through solving an ordinary annuity exercise three ways: using
Excel''s PV function, a manual mathematical formula, and a worksheet
model with flags and conditional logic to handle variable cash flow periods.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## Three ways to solve an annuity

For any annuity problem, building three independent solutions is good
practice: an Excel function, a mathematical formula, and a full
worksheet model. When all three agree, you can trust your work.

## Using the PV function

Excel''s PV function mirrors a financial calculator. The required
arguments are rate (your discount rate), nper (number of payments),
and pmt (the payment amount). Leave fv at zero for a standard
annuity, and leave type at its default of zero for an ordinary annuity
where payments fall at the end of each period.

Watch the sign convention: if you pass a positive payment, PV returns
a negative present value. Fix this by placing a minus sign in front
of the entire formula.

## The mathematical formula

The present value of a T-period annuity equals (C / r) multiplied by
(1 − 1 / (1 + r)^T). Factoring out the 1/r term first reduces the
number of parentheses you need to type and keeps the formula readable.

## Building the worksheet model with flags

Set up a row for each period up to the maximum allowed (e.g., ten
rows even if only six periods have cash flows). Add a flag column
that returns 1 when the current period is less than or equal to the
number of periods, and 0 otherwise. Multiply the payment amount by
the flag to zero out cash flows beyond the last period.

Add a discount factor column — 1 / (1 + r)^t — and multiply each
cash flow by its discount factor to get the present value. SUM the
entire present value column; the zeros take care of themselves, so
no range adjustment is needed when inputs change.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'annuities-exercise-1'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
