-- annuities-exercise-2
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('annuities-exercise-2', 'video', 'Annuities Exercise 2', 'Walks through converting an ordinary annuity to an annuity due in Excel
by switching the PV function''s type parameter from 0 to 1, adjusting the
mathematical formula by multiplying by (1 + r), and fixing the SEQUENCE
function''s start and conditional logic for beginning-of-period payments.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297532?dnt=1', 256, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('annuities-exercise-2--read', 'read', 'Annuities Exercise 2: reading', 'Walks through converting an ordinary annuity to an annuity due in Excel
by switching the PV function''s type parameter from 0 to 1, adjusting the
mathematical formula by multiplying by (1 + r), and fixing the SEQUENCE
function''s start and conditional logic for beginning-of-period payments.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## Ordinary annuity vs annuity due

The only structural difference between an ordinary annuity and an annuity
due is timing: an ordinary annuity''s first payment arrives at the end of
period one, while an annuity due''s first payment arrives immediately at
time zero. In Excel, the PV function handles this with its optional type
argument — set it to 0 (or leave it blank) for end-of-period payments,
and set it to 1 for beginning-of-period payments.

## Adjusting the PV formula

To convert your PV formula from an ordinary annuity to an annuity due,
find the type parameter at the end of the function and change it from 0
to 1. That single edit shifts all cash flows one period earlier and
produces the correct present value for an annuity due.

## Adjusting the mathematical formula

If you''re calculating the present value using a mathematical formula
rather than the built-in function, the adjustment is equally simple:
multiply the ordinary annuity result by (1 + r), where r is the
discount rate per period. This scales the value to reflect the earlier
payment timing.

## Fixing the period model with SEQUENCE

When building a cash flow timeline for an annuity due, your periods must
start at zero rather than one. Inside the SEQUENCE function, set the
start parameter to 0. Also check any conditional logic that controls how
many payments appear — because you''re now starting at zero, the condition
must use strictly less than (rather than less than or equal to) to avoid
including one extra payment.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'annuities-exercise-2'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
