-- annuities-exercise-3
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('annuities-exercise-3', 'video', 'Annuities Exercise 3', 'Works through a deferred annuity present value calculation where the first
payment starts beyond period one, building the solution with a manual
discount-factor formula and a period-flag approach using SEQUENCE and SUM.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297583?dnt=1', 465, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('annuities-exercise-3--read', 'read', 'Annuities Exercise 3: reading', 'Works through a deferred annuity present value calculation where the first
payment starts beyond period one, building the solution with a manual
discount-factor formula and a period-flag approach using SEQUENCE and SUM.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## When standard annuity formulas fall short

Excel''s built-in PV function assumes the first payment arrives at period
one (or period zero for an annuity due). When the first payment is delayed
to some later period — a deferred annuity — there is no canned Excel
formula that handles this automatically. You have to build the calculation
yourself.

## The discount adjustment formula

Start with the ordinary present value of an annuity formula: (C / r) ×
(1 − 1 / (1 + r)^n). That result is the present value as of one period
*before* the first payment. If the first payment falls in period N_start
rather than period one, multiply by an additional discount factor:
1 / (1 + r)^(N_start − 1). This brings the value back to time zero.

## Building the period-by-period model

Use SEQUENCE to generate up to 15 period rows beginning at time one. Then
create two binary flags:

- **Start flag** — equals 1 when the period is greater than or equal to
  the first payment period.
- **End flag** — equals 1 when the period is strictly less than the first
  payment period plus the number of periods.

Multiply the two flags together to get a combined flag that is 1 only
during the active payment window. Multiply each period''s cash flow by this
flag so non-payment periods automatically zero out.

## Discounting and summing

Compute the present value of $1 for each period as 1 / (1 + r)^t, then
multiply by the period''s cash flow. Sum the entire present-value column to
get the total. Because the flags force zeros outside the payment window,
you can sum all rows without worrying about including extra periods.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'annuities-exercise-3'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
