-- annuities-exercise-4
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('annuities-exercise-4', 'video', 'Annuities Exercise 4', 'Walks through the present value formula for a growing annuity that can
start at any period, then builds a spreadsheet model using raw cash flow
cells, start/end flags, and discount factors to calculate each period''s PV.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297618?dnt=1', 654, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('annuities-exercise-4--read', 'read', 'Annuities Exercise 4: reading', 'Walks through the present value formula for a growing annuity that can
start at any period, then builds a spreadsheet model using raw cash flow
cells, start/end flags, and discount factors to calculate each period''s PV.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## Growing annuity present value with a deferred start

A growing annuity pays a cash flow that increases at a constant rate each
period. Its present value formula looks like the ordinary annuity formula,
but the annuity factor uses the ratio of (1 + growth rate) to
(1 + discount rate) raised to the number of periods. Setting the growth
rate to zero collapses it back to the ordinary annuity — a useful sanity
check.

When the first payment falls in any period other than period one, multiply
the result by a discount factor of 1 / (1 + r) raised to the power of
(starting period − 1). This shifts the value back to time zero correctly.

## Building the model with helper cells

Rather than hardcoding values, the model uses a raw cash flow column.
Each cell computes the first cash flow multiplied by (1 + growth rate)
raised to (year − starting period). Because any number raised to the
power of zero equals one, the first payment period automatically shows
the exact input value, and subsequent rows grow at the correct rate.

## Controlling which periods are active with flags

Two binary flag columns determine which periods fall inside the payment
window. The start flag equals 1 when the year is greater than or equal
to the starting period; the end flag equals 1 when the year is strictly
less than (starting period + number of periods). Multiplying the two
flags together gives a single column of ones only for active payment
periods and zeros everywhere else.

The final cash flow column multiplies the raw cash flow by this combined
flag, and the discount factor column applies 1 / (1 + r) raised to the
year. Summing the present value column gives the answer, which should
match the direct formula result.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'annuities-exercise-4'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
