-- annuities-exercise-5
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, stored_at, duration_seconds, origin, source) values ('annuities-exercise-5', 'video', 'Annuities Exercise 5', 'Walks through approximating the present value of a growing perpetuity
using a 1,000-period growing annuity model, with start and end flags
to control which cash flows are included in the PV sum.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1192297710?dnt=1', 370, 'primitive', 'myrumbo:excel-for-finance (WhisperX transcript, Vimeo upload)');
insert into public.items (slug, kind, title, summary, owner_id, body_md, origin, derived_from, source) values ('annuities-exercise-5--read', 'read', 'Annuities Exercise 5: reading', 'Walks through approximating the present value of a growing perpetuity
using a 1,000-period growing annuity model, with start and end flags
to control which cash flows are included in the PV sum.', (select id from public.owners where slug = 'not-established'), '## Approximating a growing perpetuity with a long annuity

A growing perpetuity has an infinite stream of cash flows, which makes
it impossible to model row by row. The workaround is to use a very long
growing annuity — 1,000 periods — whose present value converges on the
perpetuity''s true value. The benchmark is the closed-form formula:
the first cash flow divided by (r minus g), adjusted by a discount
factor when the first payment arrives after period one.

## The timing adjustment

When the first cash flow falls in period two rather than period one,
multiply the standard perpetuity formula by 1 / (1 + r)^(start period − 1).
This discounts the entire stream back to today. The exponent is always
the starting period minus one, not the starting period itself.

## Building the model

Set up a column of 1,000 periods. The raw cash flow for each row is
the original cash flow multiplied by (1 + g)^(year − starting year),
which adjusts the growth exponent so that the series starts at the
right level regardless of when it begins.

## Controlling the cash flow window with flags

Two binary flags determine which rows are live. The start flag equals 1
when the year is greater than or equal to the starting period. The end
flag equals 1 when the year is strictly less than the starting period
plus the number of periods. Multiply the two flags together to get a
single on/off switch, then multiply the raw cash flow by that flag to
zero out rows outside the window.

## Summing to get the answer

Each live row gets multiplied by its discount factor to produce a
present value. Summing the entire present value column gives the
annuity PV, which should closely match the perpetuity formula calculated
at the top of the sheet.', 'derived', (select id from public.items where slug = 'annuities-exercise-5'), 'myrumbo descriptor prompt (read_content)');
